nio: exec path -- one-shot probe ladder (25 marks) replacing the 6-mark version

Where it stops on screen is where exec stops; the hex number printed right
after a mark is the return code of the call it follows (00000000 = success).

  __mac_execve   E02 cloneproc ret   E03 pre-imgact   E04 imgact ret
                 E05 task switched   E06 execve ret
  exec_mach_imgact
                 M01 enter  M02 pre-load  M03 load ret  M04 load ok
                 M05 platformdata  M06 sugid  M07 astrings  M08 copyout
                 M09 exit err
  load_machfile  L01 enter  L02 pre-pmap  L03 pmap ok  L04 map ok
                 L05 pre-parse  L06 parse ret
  parse_machfile P01 enter  P02 cmds ok  P03 pre-dylinker
                 P04 dylinker ret  P05 exit ret

Every mark is a standalone statement at a statement boundary: none sits inside
an argument list or a comment.  Marks go straight to the console (cons_ops[1]),
so a flooded print buffer cannot swallow them.
diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c b/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
@@ -1178,6 +1178,18 @@ nio_emark(const char *s)
 	}
 }
 
+/* nio: same writer, a hex number appended to the line already started. */
+static void
+nio_ehex(unsigned v)
+{
+	int i;
+	const char *d = "0123456789abcdef";
+
+	for (i = 28; i >= 0; i -= 4) {
+		cons_ops[1].putc(d[(v >> i) & 0xf], true);
+	}
+}
